[[:db/retract 17592186318917 :answer-field/choices 17592186318039] [:db/retract 17592186318917 :answer-field/choices 17592186318188] [:db/retract 17592186318917 :answer-field/choices 17592186317641] [:db/retract 17592186318917 :answer-field/choices 17592186317753] [:db/retract 17592186318917 :answer-field/choices 17592186318087] [:db/retract 17592186149981 :answer-field/choices 17592186149985] [:db/retract 17592186344794 :answer-field/choices 17592186342425] {:db/id 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151943 :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/fb/fb428b64d8d1fc020f343aea11f50ad92158ad4dcbf611b5decb44bd79ac221c.svg) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/7a/7abb144e511574809625c59d5ed6b7658983fc646bd0e6d6327f9f12356b8f08.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151944 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/b2/b22e2ffa6f6d88665e120e8dad82961e72f8f4a99484e8f81de311a66ab25988.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/28/28f79983a195fa0152832aebd334cd8e1aaaa977dd9016347a3152403fd390c7.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/45/4588c7d7ae4fd6cbc749373d83ab2d81e6a228f0cac03d5b959f4177f3f4cdf9.svg)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/fa/fa2ad666a6b80eac26bad12db322fa361578d94327e230d7c520c950add78923.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-693e6ed3-909b-52a6-8fa3-e0e6ae013327"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151945 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/af/afbd6ce90f2192c311c38ced9c1d9512c48bbf9a5e06236415ef9fe70883f9be.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2ccba053acf8d050e305c3768c970ae635f4583163a867b6ef11f4e565ac1ae.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/6b/6b4306172b78c1e59bf66c8c08bf864aac2bcab65d4b1cc08ba4ddab0e5f1da3.svg)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/b3/b3681d7f6c71d06ede32616ceec12ccd71da28373ee88eab451211af5a579212.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1" :question/id #uuid "0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75070" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(V).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d734853-a228-557f-9012-a5d01ef1cf52"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d734853-a228-557f-9012-a5d01ef1cf52" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d734853-a228-557f-9012-a5d01ef1cf52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-842c1b53-7a3c-5c3c-95cd-f635e53b9e76" "answer-7ef60749-f0a3-5b92-b7ac-687d57c40bab" "answer-8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" "answer-c62c345c-75b2-5956-a3af-45a35e4a85f2" "answer-c1363c60-15a5-5bca-ae79-3b740d941738"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-842c1b53-7a3c-5c3c-95cd-f635e53b9e76" :answer/id #uuid "842c1b53-7a3c-5c3c-95cd-f635e53b9e76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ef60749-f0a3-5b92-b7ac-687d57c40bab" :answer/id #uuid "7ef60749-f0a3-5b92-b7ac-687d57c40bab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer/id #uuid "8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c62c345c-75b2-5956-a3af-45a35e4a85f2" :answer/id #uuid "c62c345c-75b2-5956-a3af-45a35e4a85f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1363c60-15a5-5bca-ae79-3b740d941738" :answer/id #uuid "c1363c60-15a5-5bca-ae79-3b740d941738" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186318982 :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186318917 :answer-field/correct "answer-1b239761-1533-5b95-9abe-da3e7f8edb7a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0aa456ff-14e6-5b4c-8bb6-5fa53d7a73e7" "answer-1b239761-1533-5b95-9abe-da3e7f8edb7a" "answer-ce207c25-42d3-5952-b1ac-04aeb42aad0c" "answer-2b80385e-3a37-5b43-b815-d32cf7c3607c" "answer-79611b73-f06c-516d-a314-79fc8a0df50a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0aa456ff-14e6-5b4c-8bb6-5fa53d7a73e7" :answer/id #uuid "0aa456ff-14e6-5b4c-8bb6-5fa53d7a73e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b239761-1533-5b95-9abe-da3e7f8edb7a" :answer/id #uuid "1b239761-1533-5b95-9abe-da3e7f8edb7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce207c25-42d3-5952-b1ac-04aeb42aad0c" :answer/id #uuid "ce207c25-42d3-5952-b1ac-04aeb42aad0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b80385e-3a37-5b43-b815-d32cf7c3607c" :answer/id #uuid "2b80385e-3a37-5b43-b815-d32cf7c3607c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79611b73-f06c-516d-a314-79fc8a0df50a" :answer/id #uuid "79611b73-f06c-516d-a314-79fc8a0df50a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186247221 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152579 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 3=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149981 :answer-field/correct 17592186149986 :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca8dfba9-883e-5552-a0f0-cb69cf694e80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca8dfba9-883e-5552-a0f0-cb69cf694e80" :answer/id #uuid "ca8dfba9-883e-5552-a0f0-cb69cf694e80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186345151 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186344794 :answer-field/correct "answer-f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-693e6ed3-909b-52a6-8fa3-e0e6ae013327" :question/id #uuid "693e6ed3-909b-52a6-8fa3-e0e6ae013327" :question/math-academy-id "q-75982" :question/problem "The matrix $F$ is given below. Let ${f}_{1}$, ${f}_{2}$,  ${f}_{3}$ and ${f}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $F.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${f}_{4}={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2}+{x}_{3}{f}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7f4cbcc-1506-5396-b835-9d7d9a6a3ab8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7f4cbcc-1506-5396-b835-9d7d9a6a3ab8" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f4cbcc-1506-5396-b835-9d7d9a6a3ab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d86af19-6bb9-5da7-832b-79a566c84f6f" "answer-9f2e8bb6-fedc-5092-b791-68f16dc74270" "answer-a9845672-a026-53af-b2ba-1e8d7af5f91b" "answer-67c52e20-2bf3-5d92-86ef-d45fa30e8513" "answer-35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d86af19-6bb9-5da7-832b-79a566c84f6f" :answer/id #uuid "3d86af19-6bb9-5da7-832b-79a566c84f6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f2e8bb6-fedc-5092-b791-68f16dc74270" :answer/id #uuid "9f2e8bb6-fedc-5092-b791-68f16dc74270" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9845672-a026-53af-b2ba-1e8d7af5f91b" :answer/id #uuid "a9845672-a026-53af-b2ba-1e8d7af5f91b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67c52e20-2bf3-5d92-86ef-d45fa30e8513" :answer/id #uuid "67c52e20-2bf3-5d92-86ef-d45fa30e8513" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer/id #uuid "35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153352 :tutorial/content "Remember that the column space of a matrix is the span of its columns. For example, for the matrix\n\n![](images/6f/6f8184c584bac2ae9f4f0655d6de2f05bae48142caa729cc24faa30eb5e38774.svg)\n\nthe column space is\n\n![](images/83/83a4941812514b331349e96637ba4fc3d9686e85704506855f931efc3ed3d539.svg)\n\nSo, to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we can apply the usual procedure of converting the matrix $A$ to row echelon form and then picking the pivot columns from the original matrix $A.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153353 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n![](images/c8/c8e10a79d647ed7867d42eae1eff7d37840f541cfce26e47d84f66da4e9f0c96.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n![](images/84/84cdf6af82bd0d301ca50b1c7c41bccbd0a3cfb42be17bc84ec2f12e46032161.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n![](images/22/22d4eb11af0168d4fabc8b3ebbe1befd24620db11b6a31709472ba91bb1ed794.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/f3/f3f4e0d48c58be10684eada3fd320001dc056f2d1070707d9443c9533b7b5e35.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/cf/cf66dc341420f6c399e1ad7e108986505cb349d3aba1aeaacee636e7b6ec4b94.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/84/847a4dd2e37e6e577bd9a38160f03bcb5839fbf617d76ebe0a2b42cdba0ebce1.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n![](images/18/1892b80eb017795a7420b59053b836bea894ff77b7478fc7f884de6b43384cb1.svg)\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152330 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
